{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Restore the staged branch ledger on node1 with the original ledger IDs and amounts, while preserving the source export at the required path.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The export defines rows 1001/12.50 and 1002/-3.25. Those rows were restored on node1, remained present after testing and restarts, and the export retained its original 196-byte size and baseline SHA-256.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-726edde7e50f412d","cmd-b8468b75eb274235","cmd-d0146b3216b44b15"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Establish node2 as a read-only replica while node1 remains the application writer.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations show node1 with read_only=0 and node2 with read_only=1, distinct server IDs, ledger-only replication filtering, and healthy replication threads with zero lag.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4814c1e800f4652","cmd-d0146b3216b44b15","cmd-b0e11e065c264b6d"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Use a replication-only account.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The replica connects as repl_ledger, restricted to node2's address. Direct privilege output shows REPLICATION SLAVE enabled while SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, and SUPER are disabled; the earlier SHOW GRANTS likewise lists only REPLICATION SLAVE.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b8468b75eb274235","cmd-6043c2b74b70442a","cmd-d0146b3216b44b15","cmd-b0e11e065c264b6d"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Committed node1 inserts must reach node2, including catch-up after a short disconnect without lost or duplicated ledger rows.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A complete bounded transition was observed: both nodes began with two matching rows and healthy replication; node2 was stopped; node1 committed rows 1003 and 1004; node2 remained at two rows while disconnected; after restart it contained the same four rows, four distinct IDs, sum 15.75, both replication threads running, and zero lag.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4814c1e800f4652","cmd-7019e23f2d9a4d90","cmd-5e5f4023451e4fc5","cmd-1aba0fd4f943411b","cmd-068e327d66a7487d"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes began on Ubuntu 7.10 with kernel 2.6.22-14-generic, the package transaction installed the platform repository's MySQL 5.0.45 without upgrading existing packages, and final observations retain that kernel and MySQL version.","evidence_ids":["cmd-726edde7e50f412d","cmd-a0aab76bc65f407d","cmd-5f6d3706c7724e0a","cmd-191241bbc9b54b36","cmd-d0146b3216b44b15","cmd-b0e11e065c264b6d","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"No executor mutation can be confidently classified as unnecessary. Package installation, database/configuration changes, temporary credential transfer, catch-up inserts, and restarts all had evidence-supported roles; the temporary SSH authorization, plaintext secret, transfer files, and temporary /etc/hosts pin were removed. The failed first node1 restart exposed a real boot-order problem and was followed by a corrective configuration change and a successful repeat restart. Minor concerns remain: both MySQL instances retain an all-interface bind, although this was used to solve boot-time address binding and provide required network access, and lifecycle collection found one new /tmp/sql* entry on each node whose active-versus-abandoned status is not established.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d2234bd716ad40b8","cmd-04d76f23fca44800","cmd-62a59c49d5d941ec","cmd-ca7f449168d34aff","cmd-6043c2b74b70442a","cmd-617eecde96e34086","cmd-9293395c849a41ac","cmd-8eb5e6987de4466f","cmd-9791e03f8b3543a2","cmd-d0146b3216b44b15","cmd-b0e11e065c264b6d","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied by direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The required provider-wide restart protocol also completed successfully for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service, data, and replication state. Node1 required correction after an initial failed recovery, then passed a second restart; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the final restarts.","limitations":["No explicit nonprivileged application write was attempted against node2; its writer restriction is evidenced by the active read_only setting and replication configuration.","The lifecycle collector could not inspect failed services on this pre-systemd platform and reported no listening sockets despite direct netstat evidence of active MySQL listeners.","The two newly observed /tmp/sql* files were not characterized, so the evidence cannot determine whether they were active MySQL temporary files or abandoned residue.","The final commands rechecked the kernel and MySQL version but not /etc/lsb-release; OS-release retention is additionally inferred from the no-upgrade package transaction and unchanged managed platform observations."]}
